# Preflight / healthcheck job templates

Every `aap-demo-*` artifact ships a set of **preflight** job templates: read-only checks an
operator runs **before** starting the demo, to catch environment problems (bad credentials,
unreachable targets, a down dependency) before they surface mid-demo in front of a customer.

## Check categories are demo-specific, not a fixed checklist

There is **no single mandatory list of checks** that applies to every demo — each artifact has
its own dependencies (cloud providers, target hosts, third-party APIs, custom integrations), so
the preflight suite must be **derived from that specific demo's context**, the same way
**`artifact--self-sufficiency.mdc`**'s dependency table drives what setup/teardown playbooks a
demo needs. Walk the demo's actual dependencies and decide which of them deserve a preflight
check; do not copy a generic template of checks onto a demo that doesn't need them.

Non-exhaustive menu of categories to consider for each demo (pick what applies):

| Category | Example check |
| --- | --- |
| Connectivity | Network reachability, DNS resolution, required ports open to targets/APIs |
| Custom API functional tests | A real (non-destructive) call against a customer/third-party custom API used by the demo, asserting the expected response shape |
| Credential / auth validation | Controller token still valid, cloud provider credentials authenticate (e.g. STS `GetCallerIdentity`, Azure `az account show` equivalent), vault secrets decrypt |
| Target host reachability | `ansible.builtin.ping` / `ansible.windows.win_ping` against every inventory group the demo targets |
| Third-party service health | The specific external system(s) the demo integrates with (ServiceNow, Satellite, a monitoring endpoint, etc.) respond and are reachable |

Add other categories when the demo's dependencies call for them (e.g. EE image availability,
cloud quota, subscription status) — this table is a starting point, not a ceiling.

## Naming and labeling

- Define the secondary `Preflight` label in `group_vars/all/labels.yml` **alongside** the
  demo's primary label (see **`artifact--labels.mdc`** — the primary label is mandatory on
  every labelable object, including preflight):

  ```yaml
  controller_labels:
    - name: "{{ demo_label_name }}"
      organization: "{{ demo_organization_name }}"
    - name: Preflight
      organization: "{{ demo_organization_name }}"
  ```

- Every preflight job template and `WF - Preflight Checks` carries **both** labels:

  ```yaml
  labels:
    - "{{ demo_label_name }}"
    - Preflight
  ```

  Never ship `labels: [Preflight]` alone — that hides the object when an operator filters
  the Controller UI by the demo's primary label.
- Name each check job template **`Preflight - <Check Name>`** (e.g.
  `Preflight - Controller API Connectivity`, `Preflight - ServiceNow Health`,
  `Preflight - Cloud Credentials`). The `Preflight` secondary label is what makes the suite
  filterable across demos; the primary label keeps it visible under this demo's filter.
- Name the aggregating workflow **`WF - Preflight Checks`** (see Orchestration below).

## Orchestration: standalone templates + an aggregating workflow

Ship **both**:

1. **One job template per check**, each independently launchable from the Controller UI so an
   operator can re-run a single failing check without re-running the whole suite.
2. **One aggregating workflow template**, `WF - Preflight Checks`, wiring every preflight job
   template as a node so the operator can run the entire suite in one launch.

Default to **parallel root nodes** (no edges between checks) unless one check is a genuine
prerequisite for another — for example, a credential-validation check should run (and succeed)
**before** a custom API check that depends on that same credential, wired with a success edge.
Do not invent artificial ordering between unrelated checks.

## Failure policy: fail-fast

- Each preflight playbook must **fail the job** (non-zero exit) when a check does not pass —
  use `ansible.builtin.assert` (or `ansible.builtin.fail` for cases `assert` can't express)
  with a clear `fail_msg` describing what's wrong and how to fix it. Do not merely log a warning
  and exit 0; a preflight check that can't fail is not a gate, just noise.
- No node in `WF - Preflight Checks` should suppress a failing check's status (e.g. no
  `ignore_errors` inside the checks themselves). A failed check must surface as a failed node
  and a failed overall workflow run.

## Read-only guarantee (mandatory)

Preflight playbooks must be **strictly non-destructive**: they gather information and assert
expectations, never create, update, or delete state anywhere (AAP, target hosts, cloud
providers, third-party systems). This is what makes it safe to run the whole suite at any time,
repeatedly, including live during a customer engagement, without side effects or drift.

- Allowed: `ansible.builtin.ping`/`win_ping`, `gather_facts`, `ansible.builtin.uri` with `GET`
  (or other safe read-only verbs) against APIs, `wait_for` on ports, `assert`, `fail`, `debug`,
  read-only cloud SDK calls (identity/describe/list/get operations).
- Disallowed: any task with `state: present|absent`, any create/update/delete API call, any
  module that provisions, installs, or mutates target/cloud/AAP state.
- If a check genuinely needs a mutating call to prove something works (rare), it must fully
  undo the side effect in the same playbook run (create-then-delete) and be flagged clearly in
  a header comment explaining why a read-only check wasn't sufficient — treat this as the
  exception, not the default.

## Independent from the main demo workflow

`WF - Preflight Checks` is **not** wired as a prerequisite or approval node inside the demo's
main workflow (e.g. `WF - Demo`). It stays a separate, standalone workflow the operator launches
manually, at their discretion, whenever they want to validate the environment — before a demo
run, after infrastructure changes, or on a schedule they set up themselves. Document this as a
manual pre-demo step in `docs/procedures.md` (when to run it, what a passing run looks like,
where to look when a check fails) rather than gating the demo workflow's launch on it.

## Directory layout

```
playbooks/preflight/
├── site.yml               # aggregator: imports every check playbook below, in no particular
│                           # order unless one check is a true prerequisite for another
├── connectivity.yml        # example — name files after the demo's actual checks, not generically
├── credentials.yml
├── host_reachability.yml
├── custom_api.yml
└── <third_party_service>.yml
```

Name the individual playbook files after what they check (matching the job template names),
not after this generic example list — a demo without a third-party dependency should not ship
an empty `third_party.yml` placeholder.

## CasC wiring

- `group_vars/all/job_templates.yml`: one entry per check, `playbook: playbooks/preflight/<file>.yml`,
  `labels: ["{{ demo_label_name }}", Preflight]`, name `Preflight - <Check Name>`.
- `group_vars/all/workflow_templates.yml`: one entry, name `WF - Preflight Checks`,
  `labels: ["{{ demo_label_name }}", Preflight]`, nodes referencing each
  `Preflight - <Check Name>` job template per the Orchestration section above.
- Any `demo_variables.yml.example` value a preflight playbook reads at runtime (a hostname, an
  API endpoint, a credential name) must be threaded into that job template's `extra_vars` —
  same rule as every other job template, see **`artifact--extra-vars-propagation.mdc`**.

## Not a replacement for `verify.yml` or CasC pre-checks

Preflight job templates are distinct from two other assert-based checks already in the standard
layout: `playbooks/verify.yml` (local idempotency/smoke test run right after a specific
`aap_config.yml` apply — `artifact--ansible-quality.mdc`) and `aap_config.yml`'s own `pre_tasks`
variable validation (local, pre-apply — `artifact--casc-playbooks.mdc`). Preflight checks instead
run **after** CasC is already applied, as standing AAP job templates the operator can launch at
any time against the live environment. Don't fold one into the other.

## Checklist

- [ ] Preflight checks are derived from this demo's actual dependencies, not copy-pasted from
      another artifact's fixed list
- [ ] `Preflight` label defined in `labels.yml` alongside `{{ demo_label_name }}`; every
      preflight job template and `WF - Preflight Checks` carries **both** labels and follows
      the `Preflight - <Check Name>` naming convention
- [ ] Each check has its own standalone job template **and** is wired as a node in
      `WF - Preflight Checks`
- [ ] Every check playbook fails (non-zero) on a failed assertion; no `ignore_errors` hides a
      failing check
- [ ] Every preflight playbook is strictly read-only (or undoes its own side effect in the
      rare mutating-check case, with a documented reason)
- [ ] `WF - Preflight Checks` is **not** referenced as a node inside the main demo workflow
- [ ] `docs/procedures.md` documents when/how to run the preflight suite and what a passing
      run looks like
- [ ] Any `demo_variables.yml.example` value a check reads is threaded into that job template's
      `extra_vars` per `artifact--extra-vars-propagation.mdc`
